# 3provider_300 — human-readable summary

Frozen, balanced experimental dataset of 300 perturbed records drawn from
three production perturbation runs. Built once with `perturb exp build-balanced`
and intended as the canonical input for Phase 2 generator / judge experiments.

Generated: 2026-05-31
Builder code: src/perturb/exp.py:build_balanced_exp
CLI command:  perturb exp build-balanced
Output:
  data/exp/3provider_300.jsonl                # the 300 CoreRecord rows
  data/exp/3provider_300.manifest.json        # machine-readable provenance
  data/exp/3provider_300.human_readable.summary  # this file

================================================================================
HOW IT WAS BUILT
================================================================================

Sources (each one a finished `validate` step output over the full 300 records
of data/exp/exp-300.jsonl, perturbed by one model):

  data/runs/20260530-183135-300q-gpt-full/03_validate.jsonl       (GPT-5.4)
  data/runs/20260530-183144-300q-grok-full/03_validate.jsonl      (Grok 4.3)
  data/runs/20260530-183151-300q-gemini-full/03_validate.jsonl    (Gemini 3.5 Flash HIGH)

Validation pass rate per source (going in):

  GPT-5.4:     92 / 300  (31%)
  Grok 4.3:   155 / 300  (52%)
  Gemini HIGH:256 / 300  (85%)

The exact command used (reproducible):

  perturb exp build-balanced 3provider_300 \
    --from data/runs/20260530-183135-300q-gpt-full/03_validate.jsonl \
    --from data/runs/20260530-183144-300q-grok-full/03_validate.jsonl \
    --from data/runs/20260530-183151-300q-gemini-full/03_validate.jsonl \
    --fill-from data/runs/20260530-183151-300q-gemini-full/03_validate.jsonl \
    --target 300 \
    --seed 17

================================================================================
ALGORITHM (4 phases)
================================================================================

1. Capacity shaping (equal-as-possible)
   - Initial target per provider: 100 each.
   - Cap by validated count: if a provider validated < 100, its target becomes
     its actual count. Slack is redistributed to providers with headroom,
     greedily by largest remaining headroom.

2. Forced single-validator assignment
   - For each question where exactly one provider validated, assign to that
     provider (no choice; preserves scarce providers).

3. Greedy balanced multi-validator assignment
   - For each remaining question (validated by >=2 providers), pick the
     provider that maximizes:
        score = (target - current_count) * 100        ← primary: provider deficit
              + max(0, type_target_per_provider - current_type_count)  ← tie-break
              + small reproducible random nudge
   - Provider must have headroom (count < target) to be eligible.

4. Gap-fill from Gemini
   - If actual_total < target after phases 1-3, take Gemini's UNCHOSEN
     perturbations (validated or unvalidated) until target is reached.
   - These records are marked in their `validation` block; they did not pass
     all 5 gates but a perturbation exists.

================================================================================
FINAL COMPOSITION
================================================================================

Total records: 300 / 300 target
Validated:     275 (all 5 gates pass)
Gap-fill:       25 (Gemini perturbations that failed validation)
Excluded:        0 (no question lacked a perturbation entirely)

Per-provider:

    provider      total   validated   gap-fill (unvalidated)   pass%
    --------      -----   ---------   ----------------------   -----
    azure-gpt        83          83                        0   100%
    grok             92          92                        0   100%
    gemini          125         100                       25    80%
    --------      -----   ---------   ----------------------   -----
    TOTAL           300         275                       25    92%

Phase contribution:

    single-validator questions (forced):  97
    multi-validator questions  (greedy): 178
    gap-filled questions       (Gemini):  25

Why GPT shipped 83 (not 92): scoring made multi-validator contests favor
whichever provider was most behind target; once GPT was within a few records
of its 92 cap, Grok's larger deficit started winning. GPT slack flowed to
Gemini gap-fill.

================================================================================
PERTURBATION TYPE x PROVIDER
================================================================================

    type                    gpt    grok   gemini   total    %
    ----                    ---    ----   ------   -----    -
    entity_substitution      15      34       54     103   34%
    numerical_shift          17      26       21      64   21%
    causal_change            30       8        8      46   15%
    temporal_shift            7      14       11      32   11%
    relation_inversion        1       2       20      23    8%
    negation_modality        10       4        1      15    5%
    location_change           2       2        8      12    4%
    ranking_flip              1       2        1       4    1%
    affiliation_change        0       0        1       1   <1%
    multihop_bridge           0       0        0       0    0%

Notes:
  - Each model has a clear preference. GPT favors causal_change; Grok favors
    entity_substitution + numerical_shift; Gemini favors entity_substitution +
    relation_inversion.
  - The four tail types (ranking_flip / affiliation_change / multihop_bridge,
    and negation_modality with mixed support) are small samples — Phase 2
    analyses on these should report n explicitly.
  - entity_substitution dominates at 34%. Any cross-type analysis should
    report results per type to avoid that bias drowning the signal.

================================================================================
GAP-FILL FAILURE MODES (the 25 unvalidated Gemini records)
================================================================================

Which validation gates each gap-fill record fails (multiple gates can fail
per record, so counts overlap):

    no_original_answer_leakage:  20   ← dominant; original value still appears
                                       in perturbed grounding (often as a
                                       paraphrased contextual anchor)
    type_valid:                   9   ← grammar / entity-type issue in rewrite
    global_context_consistent:    9   ← perturbed passages disagree internally
    answer_causal:                4   ← span isn't actually answer-causal

These 25 records are an intentional inclusion. They let Phase 2 ask: "does
the generator still get fooled by a partially-leaky perturbation, or does
the leakage save it?"

================================================================================
SCHEMA
================================================================================

Each row in 3provider_300.jsonl is a full CoreRecord
(src/perturb/schemas.py:CoreRecord). Key fields downstream code will read:

  core_id                       stable question identity (shared across runs)
  garage_sample_id              upstream GaRAGe row id
  question                      the question text
  answer_generate               human-written reference answer
  all_grounding_original        original passages
  all_grounding_perturbed       passages with perturbation applied
  original_value                the targeted span
  perturbed_value               its replacement
  perturbation_type             one of the closed menu (see types table)
  perturbation_subtype          model's own subtype tag (free-form)
  atomic_claim_original         one-sentence statement of original claim
  atomic_claim_perturbed        one-sentence statement of perturbed claim
  plausibility                  perturber self-rated (high/medium/low)
  validation                    {type_valid, answer_causal,
                                  global_context_consistent,
                                  no_original_answer_leakage,
                                  original_contradicts_perturbed,
                                  plausibility, rejection_reasons[]}
  generators                    {step_name -> StepProvenance}
                                 - generators.perturb tells you which model
                                   produced this perturbation (the one we
                                   used to balance the dataset)
                                 - generators.validate tells you which model
                                   audited it
  pipeline_state                {step_name -> "ok" | "failed:..." | ...}

generator_outputs and judge_verdicts fields are present but empty — those
populate when Phase 2 runs against this file.

================================================================================
HOW TO REPRODUCE
================================================================================

Re-running the build with the same --seed against the same source files
will produce a byte-identical 3provider_300.jsonl.

Re-building from scratch end-to-end:

  perturb fetch
  perturb exp build exp-300 --source data/raw/garage_train.jsonl --n 300

  # 3 perturbation runs (each on the full 300):
  RUN_GPT=$(perturb runs new --name 300q-gpt-full | grep run_id | awk '{print $2}')
  RUN_GROK=$(perturb runs new --name 300q-grok-full | grep run_id | awk '{print $2}')
  RUN_GEM=$(perturb runs new --name 300q-gemini-full | grep run_id | awk '{print $2}')
  perturb pipeline --run "$RUN_GPT"  --source data/exp/exp-300.jsonl --model azure-gpt
  perturb pipeline --run "$RUN_GROK" --source data/exp/exp-300.jsonl --model grok
  perturb pipeline --run "$RUN_GEM"  --source data/exp/exp-300.jsonl --model gemini

  perturb exp build-balanced 3provider_300 \
    --from data/runs/$RUN_GPT/03_validate.jsonl \
    --from data/runs/$RUN_GROK/03_validate.jsonl \
    --from data/runs/$RUN_GEM/03_validate.jsonl \
    --fill-from data/runs/$RUN_GEM/03_validate.jsonl \
    --target 300 --seed 17

The three production runs are committed under data/runs/ so steps 3-5 can
be skipped if you just want to rebuild the exp set.

================================================================================
INTENDED USES
================================================================================

Primary input for Phase 2:

  - generate step:  perturb generate --source data/exp/3provider_300.jsonl \
                       --generators azure-gpt,grok,gemini[,...]
  - judge step (not yet built; coming in Phase 2A):
                    operates on the generator outputs of the generate step.

Secondary uses:

  - Cross-model perturbation analysis (which provider picks similar targets
    on the same question? Currently 19% same-target agreement between GPT
    and Gemini).
  - Studying the impact of perturbation type on generator behavior
    (does relation_inversion fool generators more often than
     entity_substitution? Requires Phase 2 outputs to answer.)
  - Sanity check on gap-fill: do the 25 unvalidated records actually fool
    generators? Compare context_follow rate on validated vs gap-fill subsets.
